      "sequence_position": 9,
      "problem": "$\\,$ $x$ is an integer or $x$ is irrational.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$ is an integer or $x$ is irrational\" is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is an integer or }x\\text{ is irrational }) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is an integer}∨x\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is an integer})∧¬(x\\text{ is irrational}) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not an integer}∧x\\text{ is not irrational } \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not an integer and }x\\text{ is not irrational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
